      correctIndex: 0,
      explanation: `<div style="border-radius:10px; box-shadow:0 4px 8px rgba(0,0,0,0.1); font-family: Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif; overflow:hidden"><div style="background-color:#01506e; border-radius:8px 8px 0 0; color:white; padding:20px"><h1 style="color:white; margin:0">Valuation Allowance for Deferred Tax Assets</h1></div><div style="background-color:white; padding:20px"><div style="background-color:#f9f9f9; border-left:5px solid #0099d4; border-radius:8px; margin-bottom:20px; padding:15px"><h3 style="color:#01506e; margin:0 0 12px 0">Correct Answer</h3><p style="margin:0"><span style="color:#4caf50; font-weight:bold">✓</span> <span style="color:#4caf50; font-weight:bold">Evidence indicates that the entity will not realize the tax benefits.</span></p></div><div style="background-color:#f0f7fa; border-left:5px solid #68d3a7; border-radius:8px; margin-bottom:20px; padding:15px"><h3 style="color:#01506e; margin:0 0 12px 0">Explanation</h3><p style="margin:0">Under ASC 740 (formerly SFAS 109), a valuation allowance must be established for deferred tax assets when it is more likely than not (more than 50% probability) that some portion or all of the deferred tax asset will not be realized. This assessment is based on the weight of available evidence, both positive and negative.</p><ul style="margin:0 0 12px 0; padding-left:22px"><li style="margin:0 0 8px 0">Key Considerations for Valuation Allowance</li><li style="margin:0 0 8px 0"><span style="color:#4caf50; font-weight:bold">✓</span> Evaluation of positive and negative evidence</li><li style="margin:0 0 8px 0"><span style="color:#4caf50; font-weight:bold">✓</span> Historical and projected future taxable income</li><li style="margin:0 0 8px 0"><span style="color:#4caf50; font-weight:bold">✓</span> Tax planning strategies available to the entity</li><li style="margin:0 0 8px 0"><span style="color:#4caf50; font-weight:bold">✓</span> Carryback and carryforward periods for tax attributes</li></ul></div><div style="background-color:#f9f9f9; border-left:5px solid #0099d4; border-radius:8px; margin-bottom:20px; padding:15px"><h3 style="color:#01506e; margin:0 0 12px 0">Analysis of Incorrect Options</h3><table cellspacing="0" style="border-collapse:collapse; margin-top:15px; width:100%"><thead><tr><th style="border-bottom:2px solid #01506e; padding:10px; text-align:left">Incorrect Option</th><th style="border-bottom:2px solid #01506e; padding:10px; text-align:left">Why It&#x27;s Incorrect</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="border-bottom:1px solid #dddddd; padding:10px">There is a change during the year in the entity&#x27;s deferred tax assets</td><td style="border-bottom:1px solid #dddddd; padding:10px">A mere change in deferred tax assets doesn&#x27;t automatically trigger a valuation allowance. The key factor is the likelihood of realization, not simply a change in the deferred tax asset balance.</td></tr><tr><td style="border-bottom:1px solid #dddddd; padding:10px">The entity plans to change the character of a taxable amount from ordinary income to capital gain in order to utilize the benefit</td><td style="border-bottom:1px solid #dddddd; padding:10px">This represents a tax planning strategy that might actually strengthen the case for realizing the deferred tax asset, not a reason to establish a valuation allowance.</td></tr><tr><td style="border-bottom:1px solid #dddddd; padding:10px">New legislation changes the applicable tax rate expected to apply in the periods in which the entity expects to realize the deferred tax asset</td><td style="border-bottom:1px solid #dddddd; padding:10px">A change in tax rates requires remeasurement of the deferred tax asset, but doesn&#x27;t necessarily require a valuation allowance. The need for a valuation allowance depends on realizability, not rate changes.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div style="background-color:#f5f9fc; border-left:5px solid #207bb5; border-radius:8px; margin-bottom:20px; padding:15px"><h3 style="color:#01506e; margin:0 0 12px 0">Journal Entry for Recording a Valuation Allowance</h3><table cellspacing="0" style="border-collapse:collapse; margin-top:15px; width:100%"><tbody><tr><td style="border-bottom:1px solid #dddddd; padding:10px">Account</td><td style="border-bottom:1px solid #dddddd; padding:10px">Debit</td><td style="border-bottom:1px solid #dddddd; padding:10px">Credit</td></tr><tr><td style="border-bottom:1px solid #dddddd; padding:10px">Income Tax Expense</td><td style="border-bottom:1px solid #dddddd; padding:10px">XXX</td><td style="border-bottom:1px solid #dddddd; padding:10px"></td></tr><tr><td style="border-bottom:1px solid #dddddd; padding:10px">Valuation Allowance for Deferred Tax Asset</td><td style="border-bottom:1px solid #dddddd; padding:10px"></td><td style="border-bottom:1px solid #dddddd; padding:10px">XXX</td></tr></tbody></table><p style="margin:0">This entry increases tax expense and reduces the net deferred tax asset to reflect the portion that is not expected to be realized.</p></div><div style="background-color:#01506e; border-radius:0 0 8px 8px; color:white; padding:15px"><h3 style="color:white; margin:0 0 12px 0">Summary</h3><p style="margin:0">A valuation allowance is required when it is more likely than not that some or all of a deferred tax asset will not be realized. This assessment requires weighing all available evidence, both positive and negative. Changes in deferred tax assets, tax planning strategies, or tax rates do not automatically trigger valuation allowances - the critical factor is always the likelihood of realization.</p></div></div></div>`,
