      correctIndex: 1,
      explanation: `<div style="border-radius:10px; box-shadow:0 4px 8px rgba(0,0,0,0.1); font-family: Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif; overflow:hidden"><div style="background-color:#01506e; border-radius:8px 8px 0 0; color:white; padding:20px"><h1 style="color:white; margin:0">Permanent Differences vs. Temporary Differences in Tax Accounting</h1></div><div style="background-color:white; padding:20px"><div style="background-color:#f9f9f9; border-left:5px solid #0099d4; border-radius:8px; margin-bottom:20px; padding:15px"><h3 style="color:#01506e; margin:0 0 12px 0">Correct Answer</h3><p style="margin:0"><span style="color:#4caf50; font-weight:bold">✓</span> <span style="color:#4caf50; font-weight:bold">$0</span></p></div><div style="background-color:#f0f7fa; border-left:5px solid #68d3a7; border-radius:8px; margin-bottom:20px; padding:15px"><h3 style="color:#01506e; margin:0 0 12px 0">Understanding Permanent Differences</h3><p style="margin:0">In this scenario, both items represent permanent differences between book (financial) income and taxable income:</p><p style="margin:0">Premium on officer&#x27;s life insurance ($15,000): When a company is the owner and beneficiary of a life insurance policy, the premiums paid are not tax-deductible for tax purposes, but are recorded as expenses on the financial statements.</p><p style="margin:0">Interest received on municipal bonds ($20,000): This interest income is included in financial income but is exempt from federal income tax.</p><p style="margin:0">Key Concept: Permanent differences do not reverse over time and do not create deferred tax assets or liabilities. They simply cause the effective tax rate to differ from the statutory tax rate.</p><ul style="margin:0 0 12px 0; padding-left:22px"><li style="margin:0 0 8px 0">Tax Calculation Analysis</li><li style="margin:0 0 8px 0">Book vs. Tax Income Reconciliation</li></ul><table cellspacing="0" style="border-collapse:collapse; margin-top:15px; width:100%"><thead><tr><th style="border-bottom:2px solid #01506e; padding:10px; text-align:left">Item</th><th style="border-bottom:2px solid #01506e; padding:10px; text-align:left">Amount</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="border-bottom:1px solid #dddddd; padding:10px">Pretax financial income</td><td style="border-bottom:1px solid #dddddd; padding:10px">$100,000</td></tr><tr><td style="border-bottom:1px solid #dddddd; padding:10px">Add: Life insurance premium (non-deductible)</td><td style="border-bottom:1px solid #dddddd; padding:10px">$15,000</td></tr><tr><td style="border-bottom:1px solid #dddddd; padding:10px">Less: Municipal bond interest (tax-exempt)</td><td style="border-bottom:1px solid #dddddd; padding:10px">$(20,000)</td></tr><tr><td style="border-bottom:1px solid #dddddd; padding:10px">Taxable income</td><td style="border-bottom:1px solid #dddddd; padding:10px">$95,000</td></tr><tr><td style="border-bottom:1px solid #dddddd; padding:10px">Current tax expense (30% × $95,000)</td><td style="border-bottom:1px solid #dddddd; padding:10px">$28,500</td></tr><tr><td style="border-bottom:1px solid #dddddd; padding:10px">Deferred tax liability</td><td style="border-bottom:1px solid #dddddd; padding:10px">$0</td></tr></tbody></table><p style="margin:0">Note: Since both differences are permanent, there are no future reversals to account for. Therefore, no deferred tax liability is recorded.</p></div><div style="background-color:#01506e; border-radius:0 0 8px 8px; color:white; padding:15px"><h3 style="color:white; margin:0 0 12px 0">Summary</h3><p style="margin:0">Lake Corp. has two permanent differences that affect the calculation of taxable income but do not create timing differences:</p><p style="margin:0">The life insurance premium ($15,000) increases taxable income relative to book income.</p><p style="margin:0">The municipal bond interest ($20,000) decreases taxable income relative to book income.</p><p style="margin:0">Conclusion: Since these are permanent differences, they do not result in future taxable or deductible amounts. Therefore, no deferred tax liability should be reported on Lake&#x27;s December 31 balance sheet.</p></div></div></div>`,
